WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
One gamer used ChatGPT to build custom mods that led him shapeshift into monsters and gears of war. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 207, The Deep Dive, Job Hunting with a Chatbot, new labels on computer-made ads, and a week-and-review come after this. A Gears of War fan posted on Reddit that he first watched the trailers while working mall security in 2017. He opened ChatGPT, the Chatbot many people already used for homework and questions, specifically a version called

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:36.080
GPT-6 Astra. In everyday words, he described features he had wanted for years, including

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.709
Beast Mode, a way to fight as the creatures that later games never brought back. A mod

00:00:41.709 --> 00:00:46.889
is a homemade change to a game, like resticking Lego bricks so a castle becomes a spaceship.

00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:52.669
Each week he tried tiny bits of instructions the chatbot offered, told it what crashed and

00:00:52.669 --> 00:00:57.969
requested the next week. Code is a list of instructions a computer follows, the same way

00:00:57.969 --> 00:00:59.949
Their recipe tells you each kitchen step.

00:01:00.696 --> 00:01:04.316
The creatures kept their original attacks, and he could finally control them.

00:01:04.756 --> 00:01:10.176
The project grew to cover Gears of War III on the Jacinto map, Gears V and Gears of

00:01:10.176 --> 00:01:10.856
War II Day.

00:01:11.476 --> 00:01:18.056
Gears V alone got 365 different character looks, new weapon combinations, tweaks to Horde mode,

00:01:18.556 --> 00:01:20.216
and matches against offline bots.

00:01:20.916 --> 00:01:25.636
Horde mode is a survival match against waves of enemies, and bots are computer controlled

00:01:25.636 --> 00:01:26.736
teammates or opponents.

00:01:26.736 --> 00:01:32.496
All of it runs through one program he named Gears Toybox, which works like a TV remote

00:01:32.496 --> 00:01:33.796
for three games at once.

00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:40.236
He picks any of the three, sets up a match, and uses one F10 key, a keyboard shortcut that

00:01:40.236 --> 00:01:42.756
shuts the current game and drops him back at the menu.

00:01:43.256 --> 00:01:47.176
Play stays on his own machine and never connects to public multiplayer servers.

00:01:47.656 --> 00:01:51.716
That is like sitting around a kitchen table with a board game instead of joining strangers

00:01:51.716 --> 00:01:52.416
on the internet.

00:01:52.856 --> 00:01:55.816
The result feels like official, extra downloadable content.

00:01:55.816 --> 00:01:58.356
The add-on stuff companies usually sell.

00:01:58.816 --> 00:02:02.456
It still came from everyday sentences instead of a programming classroom.

00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:05.436
Open chat GPT on your phone or laptop.

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:10.816
Describe a small game change you wish existed even in a board game you own, and ask it to

00:02:10.816 --> 00:02:12.076
walk you through the first step.

00:02:13.043 --> 00:02:17.843
The reason a chat box can spit out working instructions hides in a trick your phone already knows.

00:02:18.503 --> 00:02:21.863
While you text, the little bar above the keys offers the next word.

00:02:21.863 --> 00:02:26.623
It uses your half-finished sentence and habits learned from a huge pile of other chats.

00:02:27.103 --> 00:02:31.343
Stretch that guessing game until it predicts whole lines of instructions that tell a computer

00:02:31.343 --> 00:02:32.183
what to do next.

00:02:33.023 --> 00:02:36.623
During training, the system read billions of lines of existing game code.

00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:42.223
Training means it studied huge piles of examples until the patterns stuck, like learning song

00:02:42.223 --> 00:02:43.903
lyrics by hearing them over and over.

00:02:44.503 --> 00:02:49.163
First, you describe the change in ordinary words, such as make the character jump higher.

00:02:49.163 --> 00:02:53.523
Next, it predicts the most likely chunks of instructions that would cause that jump.

00:02:54.103 --> 00:02:56.523
It does not invent brand new rules from thin air.

00:02:56.983 --> 00:02:59.163
It rearranges patterns it has seen before.

00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:02.903
The way a musician remixes familiar chords into a new song.

00:03:03.563 --> 00:03:06.783
Then you try the result, say what broke, and it guesses a fix.

00:03:07.363 --> 00:03:11.503
That back and forth is like editing a group project with a classmate who keeps offering

00:03:11.503 --> 00:03:12.323
better sentences.

00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:16.543
After that, you repeat and each round of feedback narrows the possibilities.

00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:21.123
The same way repeated talks with a friend help them understand exactly what you mean.

00:03:21.763 --> 00:03:25.743
The chatbot handles the heavy pattern matching while you steer with ordinary language,

00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:28.363
so special symbols are not the price of entry.

00:03:28.923 --> 00:03:31.363
The loop stays a conversation from start to finish.

00:03:31.903 --> 00:03:36.403
Once you see remixing plus feedback, the whole trick has a kitchen table explanation.

00:03:37.422 --> 00:03:41.182
Chat GPT is being used as a personal job scout for summer work,

00:03:41.622 --> 00:03:44.202
college majors, and careers that are hard to even name.

00:03:44.202 --> 00:03:51.622
One Reddit user set it up to read job posts, line them up with what the person can do and fire off applications on its own.

00:03:52.142 --> 00:03:56.282
Picture a career counselor who never clocks out and never rolls their eyes at a question.

00:03:56.942 --> 00:04:04.662
On the chat GPT website or the mobile app, start a new chat and type that you are a high school student who likes video editing and helping people.

00:04:05.302 --> 00:04:10.522
Ask it to suggest five realistic part-time jobs and write a short cover letter for the first one.

00:04:10.522 --> 00:04:14.962
keep chatting to refine the results, change the tone of the letter, or request

00:04:14.962 --> 00:04:19.562
salary ranges and required skills. Later, that same chat can remind you of

00:04:19.562 --> 00:04:22.842
deadlines or help prepare answers for common interview questions.

00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:28.533
Pennsylvania now requires labels on ads that use artificial intelligence or AI

00:04:28.533 --> 00:04:32.173
to change or create images, video, audio, or text.

00:04:32.693 --> 00:04:36.773
The Verge reports the rule is meant to stop sneaky fake endorsements or altered

00:04:36.773 --> 00:04:42.253
photos that trick people. Starting soon, any ad in that state built or change this way must

00:04:42.253 --> 00:04:47.713
clearly say so. The law covers a still image, a short video clip, or even a voiceover that

00:04:47.713 --> 00:04:52.853
sounds like a real person endorsing a product. The label works like the nutrition box on a snack,

00:04:53.273 --> 00:04:57.933
because it does not stop you from looking, it just tells you what went into it. Over time,

00:04:58.053 --> 00:05:02.353
those labels may feel familiar when you scroll social media or watch YouTube ads there.

00:05:02.353 --> 00:05:08.275
Block, the company behind the Payment App Cash App, released a speech model called Desa.

00:05:08.535 --> 00:05:11.335
A model here is the train program doing the listening.

00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:16.775
The decoder says Desa processes audio directly instead of turning speech into text first,

00:05:17.235 --> 00:05:22.755
so it can pick up sarcasm, pauses, and emotion in real time, which regular chatbots often

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:22.995
miss.

00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:27.275
That is like hearing a friend and person instead of reading a transcript of the call.

00:05:27.275 --> 00:05:33.795
It runs locally on a MacBook Pro M4 Max, meaning the laptop itself does the work, and it

00:05:33.795 --> 00:05:36.835
is built to catch feelings that vanish when voice becomes words.

00:05:37.906 --> 00:05:43.366
A lawsuit now asks the judge to block miners from using chat GPT, and Texas is the state

00:05:43.366 --> 00:05:44.086
that filed it.

00:05:44.546 --> 00:05:49.546
The filing, described on Reddit, claims the company did not warn users enough about possible

00:05:49.546 --> 00:05:50.426
harmful answers.

00:05:50.946 --> 00:05:55.206
It asks the court to require an independent review of new models before they reach Texas

00:05:55.206 --> 00:05:55.666
users.

00:05:55.666 --> 00:06:02.286
It also seeks more than $1 million in penalties, plus limits on features that might encourage compulsive use.

00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:07.753
Across the last seven days, other experiments sat beside that Gears of War project.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:12.733
Artificial Intelligence agents, which are programs that can take actions not just answers,

00:06:13.333 --> 00:06:16.493
teamed up to beat Minecraft's final boss with zero human help.

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:22.133
Separate systems designed brand new proteins, the tiny building blocks inside living things,

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:27.793
to fight deadly scorpion venom, and eighth-grader put three major AIs through an empathy test,

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:31.773
a check on whether they notice feelings, and found they often miss the mark.

00:06:32.353 --> 00:06:33.213
That's it for today.

00:06:33.533 --> 00:06:37.133
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:06:37.553 --> 00:06:40.753
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:06:41.113 --> 00:06:42.513
That's literally how learning works.

00:06:43.093 --> 00:06:46.053
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

